{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789214457,"nonce":"AiVU0jnFFaXSnaRDqXBXGES4+DU=","title":"Dict and set as in-language hash tables, not FFI","message":"Python's dict and set need a keyed container. The prelude reserves the\nslot (`// extern struct HashMap\u003cK, V\u003e` behind a TODO) and a working\nimplementation exists in a sibling runtime with the right storage\nmodel: raw 64-bit values, no per-value boxing, key type known at\ncompile time. Neither is the answer as it stands.\n\nBehind a symbol call, every access is a memory barrier: load_cse and\nLICM must assume every value they hold about memory is stale after\n`Call`, so a dictionary read inside a hot loop does not just cost the\ncall, it stops everything around it from being hoisted or shared.\nTensor started behind FFI and moved its arithmetic in-language for\nexactly this reason; the kernels that matter are explicitly no-FFI and\nthat is where their speedup came from.\n\nSo: an open-addressing table written in the language, over two Lists\nand a state byte, with the hash and the probe loop in-language so the\ninliner, LICM and CSE see through every access. Monomorphised by key\ntype, `dict[str, int]` becomes a table of unboxed i64 with no tags,\nwhich is where compiled Python's speedup over CPython lives.\n\nSteps: (1) `extern struct HashMap\u003cK, V\u003e` first so the type exists and\nthe frontend can land, (2) the in-language table replaces it, (3) the\nsibling's map stays as a correctness oracle for the table's tests and\nas the storage model to copy. `dict[Any, Any]` hashes through\nDynamicBox and is the exception the type checker forces you into, not\nthe default.\n\nSet is the same table with no value column.","files":null}]}